.TH PSDataFromText 3 2024-03-09 "PsyC 0.9.5" "PsyC Library Functions Manual"

.SH NAME
\fBPSDataFromText\fR

.SH LIBRARY
PsyC (-lpsyc)

.SH SYNOPSIS

.B
#include <psyc/dataset.h>

.I
PSFloat  *
.br
\fBPSDataFromText\fR(\fIchar *str\fR, \fIPSTextParserOptions *opts\fR, \fIlong *datalen\fR, \fIPSVocabulary ** vocabulary\fR)
.SH DESCRIPTION
Load a dataset (an array of \fIPSFloat\fR numbers) from a string. Depending on the parsing mode, each token or character found in the string will be converted to a numeric representation of itself. The dataset can be used to train a model (\fIPSModel\fR) or it can provide inputs to the model.
.br
Numeric representation of tokens/characters is defined by key-value pairs contained into \fIvocabulary\fR and indices (ids) related to token are cast to \fIPSFloat\fR.
.br
The function can use an existing vocabulary or create a new one from scratch.
.br
By default, the string will be parsed as a sequence of tokens (\fIPS_PARSER_MODE_TOKENS\fR) and each token will be converted to a number.
.br
The above behavior can be changed by using \fIPS_PARSER_MODE_CHARS\fR in the (optional) \fIopts\fR argument (see \fIPSTextParserOptions\fR).
.br

.br
When \fIPS_PARSER_MODE_TOKENS\fR mode is used, tokens can be matched in two ways:
.br

.RS 4
\[bu] By using a separator: in this case the string will be split by using the separators defined into \fIseparator\fR member of \fIopts\fR. If \fIseparator\fR is \fINULL\fR or \fIopts\fR is \fINULL\fR, the default separators will be those defined by the macro \fIPS_DEFAULT_TOKEN_SEPARATOR\fR.
.br

\[bu] By using a callback that let the developers to define their own logic for identifying and extracting tokens from the input string. The callback function can be set into the \fImatch_token\fR member of \fIopts\fR, and it's a function of type \fIPSTokenMatch\fR. The callback receives a token to match and a pointer to an integer to store the length of the matched token. If the callback returns a non-zero value (true), it indicates a successful match and a token of the matched length will be extracted from the input string. In this case, the parsing position is moved forward by the matched length. If the callback returns 0, it signals that the token was not matched, and the parsing position will be advanced to the next byte.
.RE
.RE


.br
If the flag \fIPS_PARSER_FLAG_ENCODE_ONLY\fR is set, the resulting dataset will only consist of converted token values, with no further info or metadata about the dataset itself. Otherwise, other data is added to the dataset, such as sequence length, number of sequences, and so on.
.br

.br
By default, the dataset is generated as a whole single sequence, and the sequence length is prepended as the first element of the dataset.
.br
In order to split text into multiple sequences, it's possible to use the properties of \fIopts\fR:
.br

.RS 4
\[bu] If \fIsequence_length\fR is greater than zero, the text will be split into into multiple fixed-length sequence having \fIsequence_length\fR tokens.
.br

\[bu] If the \fImatch_sequence_end\fR callback is not \fINULL\fR, it will be called with the current token and, if its return value is true, the token will be the ending token of the current sequence. It produces variable-length sequences.
.br

\[bu] If the \fIsequence_separator\fR string is not \fINULL\fR and the current token is equal to it, the current token will be the ending token of the current sequence. It produces variable-length sequences.
.RE
.RE

When the dataset is split into multiple sequences, the total number of sequence is set into the first element of the dataset itself.
.br

.br
By default, no target data will be generated, unless the flag \fIPS_PARSER_FLAG_MAKE_TARGETS\fR is set into \fIflags\fR member of \fIopts\fR or a target dataset is specified into \fIopts\fR by using the \fItarget_dataset\fR and the \fItarget_datalen\fR members of \fIopts\fR.
.br
If no \fItarget_dataset\fR is provided, by default the target sequences will be generated by shifting the related input sequence by one. For example, if we have an the following sequence "hello world have a nice day" and a \fIsequence_length\fR of five tokens, the input sequence will be "hello world have a nice" and the target sequence will be "world have a nice day".
.br
Incomplete sequences are dropped, but it's possible to "pad" them by using 'start' and 'end' tokens that can be set with the \fIPS_PARSER_FLAG_START_TOKEN\fR and \fIPS_PARSER_FLAG_END_TOKEN\fR flags into \fIflags\fR member of \fIopts\fR.
.br
If not specified by \fIstart_token\fR and \fIend_token\fR members of \fIopts\fR, the function will use the default string defined by \fIPS_DEFAULT_START_TOKEN\fR and \fIPS_DEFAULT_END_TOKEN\fR.
.br
If a \fItarget_dataset\fR is provided, target sequences will be taken from it (the target dataset must contain at least the same number of sequences of the current dataset being generated).
.br

.br
\fBWARN\fR:  The parsed string \fIstr\fR may be modified during text parsing. By setting the \fIPS_PARSER_FLAG_PRESERVE_STRING\fR flag into `opts->flags`, the function will work on a copy of the string, preventing the original string from being altered.
.br

.br
.B ARGUMENTS
.br

.RS 4
.TP
.B str
the (null-terminated) string to be parsed (mandatory).
.br

.TP
.B opts
parsing options, it can be \fINULL\fR.
.br

.TP
.B existing_data
optional argument that can be used to append    parsed data to an existing dataset. WARN: Since data can be reallocated, always use the returned dataset after calling the function.
.br

.TP
.B datalen
pointer to \fIint64_t\fR where the final length of the dataset    will be stored. If \fIexisting_data\fR is not \fINULL\fR, the address pointed by \fIdatalen\fR must contain the current length of the existing dataset. If \fINULL\fR is returned by the function, the pointed address will contain zero.
.br

.TP
.B vocabulary
pointer to pointer to a \fIPSVocabulary\fR struct. The    argument is mandatory and cannot be \fINULL\fR. If the pointer pointed by \fIvocabulary\fR is \fINULL\fR, a new \fIPSVocabulary\fR will be allocated and it will be filled with parsed tokens|characters. If the pointer pointed by \fIvocabulary\fR points to an already existing vocabulary, its numeric values will be used for parsed tokens. If a token or character is not found in the existing vocabulary, it will be automatically added, unless \fIPS_PARSER_FLAG_READONLY_VOCAB\fR flag is set into \fIopts\fR.
.RE
.RE


.SH RETURN VALUES
The dataset (\fIPSFloat\fR array) or \fINULL\fR is something goes wrong.

.SH SEE ALSO
psyc(1)
